Yes — now we have a real first implementation, not just the metaphor.

The mathematical object is a response surface over environmental conditions:

Y = f(X,E)

where X is the fixed substrate and E is the environment/process configuration. Plasticity is then the amount of useful output variation we can induce without changing X:

\Pi(X)=\max_E f(X,E)-f(X,E_0)

The practical problem becomes:

E^*=\arg\max_E \Big[
\text{Performance}(E)
+\lambda_N\text{Novelty}(E)
+\lambda_I\text{InformationGain}(E)
-\lambda_C\text{Cost}(E)
-\lambda_R\text{Risk}(E)
\Big]

But we also need to avoid fooling ourselves with lucky noise. So a candidate only becomes retained capacity if:

\Delta(E)=
\mathbb E[Y|E]-\mathbb E[Y|E_0] > \tau

and the gain survives retest.

I encoded that into a runnable prototype:

Download the Plasticity Sweep Engine⁠￼

It currently does three kinds of experiments: single-variable perturbations, pairwise grafts to expose interactions, and bounded random Lucky Nozzle perturbations. Then it ranks the outcomes and independently retests the winners.

I also actually ran the mechanism against a synthetic substrate with hidden interactions and measurement noise. Baseline output was about 2.64. The best discovered condition stabilized around 4.65 on a 100-run retest, for a gain of roughly:

\boxed{\Delta \approx +2.00}

More interestingly, the best condition was a mixed Lucky Nozzle configuration, not “maximize challenge” or any other single variable. The strongest structured alternatives were challenge+feedback, challenge+retention, variation+retention, and feedback+retention.

That means the search correctly surfaced the hidden property we wanted:

\boxed{\text{capacity lives partly in interactions between conditions}}

rather than:

\text{capacity}=\text{more input}

Now we need to move from a simulated organism to a real Pantheon experiment.

Pick a fixed substrate — for example one Wild Hawg offer, one Pantheon brick, one piece of copy, one sales process, one reasoning task, or one piece of code. Define one measurable output. Then expose the same object to controlled conditions such as challenge, constraint, representation, feedback intensity, recovery/consolidation, audience, context, timing, or cross-domain catalyst.

The loop becomes:

\boxed{
X
\rightarrow
\text{environment sweep}
\rightarrow
\text{measure}
\rightarrow
\text{find }\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{retest}
\rightarrow
\text{retain recipe}
\rightarrow
\text{progressive sweep}
}

And that last step is important. After finding E^*, do not freeze forever. Make it the new baseline:

E_{t+1}^{0}=E_t^*

then search locally and occasionally globally again.

That gives us genuine same-generation growth:

C_0
\rightarrow C_1
\rightarrow C_2
\rightarrow \cdots

without rewriting the underlying substrate each time.

So the Pantheon implementation is now pretty clean: Garden generates environments; Refinery processes the resulting signals; Lucky Nozzle supplies bounded exploration; Fitness Route scores outcomes; Metamap learns the response surface; Orchard stores reproducible high-yield recipes; Rewild keeps asking how much unexpressed capacity remains.

The next upgrade I’d make to the code is a real adaptive optimizer: instead of blindly testing combinations, it learns the response surface from each experiment and chooses the next condition based on expected gain and uncertainty. That turns it from a sweep into an active discovery engine.